===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sit down.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|<,,sat:sate[dated, poetic],sat:sitten[archaic, dialectal]>}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To assume a sitting position from a standing position.
#: {{ux|en|'''Sit down'''! We have work to do.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''sat down''' in the armchair, and my sis '''sat down''' on the floor.}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|book=Matthew|chapter=9|verse=10|folio=xj|passage=And lo&nbsp;/ many publicans and ſynners&nbsp;/ cam and '''ſatt downe''' alſo with Ieſus&nbsp;/ and his diſciples.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|tlr=Ihon Daus|title=A Famouse Cronicle of Oure Time, Called Sleidanes Commentaries, Concerning the State of Religion and Common Wealth,{{nb...|During the Raigne of the Emperour Charles the Fift, with the Argumentes Set Before Euery Booke, Conteyninge the Summe or Effecte of the Booke Following. Translated out of Latin into Englishe, by Ihon Daus. Here vnto Is Added Also an Apology of the Authoure.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Imprinted at London, by}} Ihon Daye, for Abraham Veale, and Nicholas England|date=25 September 1560|section=folio cccxij, verso|passage=From thence he cometh forth again ⁊ '''ſitteth down''' vnder his cloth of eſtate.}}
#* {{RQ:Plutarch North Lives|chapter=The Life of ''Marcus Antonius''|page=1001|passage=When ſhe knew this galley a farre of, ſhe lift vp a ſigne in the poope of her ſhippe, and ſo ''Antonius'' comming to it, was pluckt vp where ''Cleopatra'' was, howbeit he ſaw her not at his firſt comming, nor ſhe him, but went and '''ſate down''' alone in the prowe of his ſhippe, and ſaid neuer a word, clapping his head betwene both his hands.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=&#91;[[w:William Bradshaw (Puritan)|William Bradshaw]]&#93;|title=A Proposition. Concerning Kneeling in the Very Act of Receiuing Howsoever.{{nb...|Published to Satisfie Professours, yet Humblie, Submitted to the Iudgment of Prophets.}}|year=1605|page=13|passage=Therfore where it is ſuppoſed that Chriſt and his Apoſtles miniſtred & receiued ſitting but by occaſion, and not of purpoſe: becauſe they were ſitting before in eating the paſſeover. wher as if Chriſt had '''ſitten down''' of purpoſ to adminiſter the cõmunion, then all that is ſaid is graunted to be ſome purpoſe.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Galliazzo Gualdo Priorato|tlr=[[w:Henry Carey, 2nd Earl of Monmouth|Henry [Carey, 2nd] Earle of Monmouth]]|title=An History of the Late Warres and Other State Affaires of the Best Part of Christendom, Beginning with the King of Swethlands Entrance into Germany, and Continuing to the Yeare 1640|location=London|publisher=W. Wilson, and are to bee sold by Iohn Hardesty, Thomas Huntington, and Thomas Iackson,{{nb...|at their shops in Ducklane}}|year=1648|pages=340–341|passage={{...}} he made ''Maracini'' advance with his men towards ''Oder'', to hinder ''Orang hells'' further proceedings, or his joyning with ''Bannier'', which if it had happened time enough, that Elector would have had ſomewhat elſe to doe, then to have '''ſitten down''' before a ſtrong Towne which had a prettie Army in the Field to aſſiſt it.}}
